            <ArticleTitle>Data on internal cDNA amplification and color changes of the proteins derived from Pacific white leg shrimp shell.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>105-108</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.dib.2017.11.025</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this article, we report original data on the designation of the primers for full-length cDNA amplification and the internal cDNA amplification of red color-related pigment-binding protein derived from shrimp shell. Data on the color shifts of different soluble proteins under 100 °C 10 min heat treatment and the effects of heating temperatures (from 30 to 100 °C) on the color changes of crude water-soluble proteins are also included in this report. For further details and experimental findings please refer to the article &quot;Isolation and cDNA cloning of a novel red color-related pigment-binding protein derived from the shell of shrimp, Litopenaeus vannamei&quot; (Chuang et al., 2017) [1].</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
